herdr is the terminal workspace manager for AI coding agents.
The current config uses prefix Ctrl+J (the built-in default is Ctrl+B).
Prefix notation means press the prefix, release it, then press the command key.

Current configuration:
	Prefix - Ctrl+j
	Mouse capture - Enabled (Herdr handles pane clicks and scrolling)
	Theme - tokyo-night; agent labels on pane borders - Enabled

Prefix actions (default actions, with Ctrl+j substituted for prefix):
	Prefix ? - Show key help
	Prefix s - Open settings
	Prefix q - Detach from the workspace
	Prefix Shift+r - Reload configuration
	Prefix o - Open the notification target
	Prefix w - Workspace picker; Prefix g - Goto
	Prefix Shift+n - Create workspace
	Prefix Shift+g - Create worktree; Prefix e - Edit scrollback
	Prefix c - New tab; Prefix Shift+t - Rename tab
	Prefix p/n - Previous/next tab; Prefix 1-9 - Switch tab
	Prefix Shift+x - Close tab; Prefix Shift+p - Rename pane
	Prefix h/j/k/l - Focus pane left/down/up/right
	Prefix Tab / Shift+Tab - Cycle to next/previous pane
	Prefix v - Split vertically; Prefix - - Split horizontally
	Prefix x - Close pane; Prefix z - Zoom/fullscreen pane
	Prefix r - Enter resize mode; Prefix b - Toggle sidebar

Navigate mode:
	h/j/k/l - Move to pane left/down/up/right
	Arrow keys - Move through workspace navigation where shown

Useful CLI equivalents:
	herdr status - Show client/server status
	herdr workspace list - List workspaces
	herdr tab list - List tabs
	herdr pane list - List panes
	herdr pane read <pane> - Read pane output
	herdr pane focus --pane <pane> --direction left|right|up|down - Focus a neighbor
	herdr pane split <pane> - Split a pane

Remote sessions use the same prefix actions unless overridden by
`--remote-keybindings local|server`. Run `herdr --default-config` to inspect
all optional bindings before adding them to `~/.config/herdr/config.toml`.
